**Read:** [Sampling contributions, thresholds, and results near zero](../../../inquiry/metrology/uncertainty/Sampling-and-censoring.lingua#r_3RRJRMNNAPMVYJ9Z6VGY2DV0CG), sampling and nested variation; [Chemical measurements — matrix, recovery, selectivity, and reference material](../../../inquiry/metrology/applications/Chemical-and-reference-material-measurements.lingua#r_78FAVM8QZ3F7N4DQP2DDP1R7KQ), specimen-to-result accounting.

Repeated readings of one prepared specimen do not provide independent evidence about the whole target. A simple hierarchical model is y_ijk = μ + a_i + b_ij + ε_ijk, where a_i represents sampled-region variation, b_ij preparation variation within region and ε_ijk analytical variation. Specify independence and variance assumptions before using this decomposition.

For a balanced design with n independent regions, p preparations per region and r readings per preparation, var(mean) = σ_a²/n + σ_b²/(np) + σ_ε²/(npr). If the respective variances are 9, 4 and 1 in squared result units and n = 3, p = 2, r = 4, the mean variance is 3.708333. Increasing only repeated readings eventually does little to reduce the dominant region contribution.

These terms describe a declared population and design. Targeted regions, correlated specimens or destructive preparation can require another model. Spatial heterogeneity is sometimes the property of interest rather than noise to average away.

**Work:** Derive the mean variance by counting shared terms and compare allocating effort to more regions, more preparations or more readings. Build a small simulated hierarchy and show how flattening all observations into independent replicates understates uncertainty. Preserve the hierarchy when reporting the result.

